-- `battle.json field.melee` (army_field.gd _press, man to man at blows, Total War's melee; Mac 2026-11-16: "there is no proper fight", "the press looks like a mob"): only the men who can reach a foe step out of their ranks, the rest hold their places. pace 0.35 (a loose man's walk, horse twice), engage 0.3 (how near a foe man must be for him to go at him; a man in his rank steps out at 1.5 times it), reach 0.09 (a weapon's length he stops at), leash 0.22 (the furthest he goes from his place), loosen_s 0.3 (seconds between reads of who else steps out). Archers: `field.volley.arc` 50 (degrees off its front a mark may stand before the block wheels onto it; within it the block holds its front).
+- `battle.json field.melee` (army_field.gd _press, a block's fighting shape at blows; Mac 2026-11-16: "the first two rows are fighting, so the rows behind it will move on the sides ... the soldiers in the troop need to move ahead to fill in those gaps"): reach 0.09 (a weapon's length between the two front rows), rank 1.2 (a row's depth, of a file's width), read 0.3 (seconds between reads of the shape: who fell, which sides are open), shout 0.04 (chance each read that a man not at blows shouts or raises his shield). Horse stays at blows until its mark breaks (`field.cavalry.melee_s` is gone). Archers: `field.volley.arc` 50 (degrees off its front a mark may stand before the block wheels onto it). Mac 2026-11-16 "armies should not be able to walk through each other", "when they are at a certain vicinity, they cannot be walking, they have to be attacking": body 0.08 (the ground one man takes up; no man steps onto ground another stands on), near 0.14 (a foe man this near: he stops walking and fights him). `field.bounds.margin` 0.6 (the battle's boundary: this far outside the box round both hosts' foot; no ride or pull-back goes beyond it; Mac 2026-11-16: "make sure the troops are not moving out of the boundary of the host"). The horse follows its foot into the fight (Mac 2026-11-16: "the cavalry then follows them ... everyone needs to be bloodthirsty for war"): every order keeps its horse behind the foot (`battle_orders.json` horse "reserve"), and it goes in round the end of its line the moment the lines lock; `field.follow` behind 0.9 (how far behind the back of their own foot the archers keep station), horse 0.3 (clear ground the horse keeps behind its side's rearmost block); `field.cavalry.wide` 0.4. `field.melee.touch` 0.8 (any two enemy blocks whose middles are nearer than this share of their half sizes together are locked at blows; Mac 2026-11-16: "the cavalry of the 2 armies just went through each other"). `field.melee.per_foe` 2 (at most this many of a block's men go at one foe man; the rest find their own further along), `field.melee.seek` 3.0 (how far off a foe block at blows may be for its men to be gone at); Mac 2026-11-16: "everyone's goal needs to be to get a piece of the action", "why they are clustering in one place". A man keeps the foe man he has taken on until that man is down (r.quarry); riders one to a man. Horse (Mac 2026-11-16: "reduce the number of cavalry members that we have and increase the spacing"): `army.json units.horse` men 60 (12 riders drawn), spacing file 0.42, rank 0.64. `field.melee.pace` 0.28 (how fast a man steps in the press), `horse_pace` 1.4 (a rider's, times that); Mac 2026-11-16: "why are the horses running so much".
